  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n\nWe improve upon the original program by incorporating a symmetry constraint and multi-resolution optimization. The original problem minimizes the overlap integral over step functions on [0,2] with integral 1. The overlap function is symmetric, suggesting that the optimal solution might be symmetric: h(x) = 1 - h(2-x). We enforce this symmetry to reduce the degrees of freedom by half. Additionally, we introduce a multi-resolution optimization strategy: \n\n1. Coarse-grid initialization: Start with a small grid (256 points) to find a good local minimum quickly\n2. Multi-resolution refinement: Gradually increase grid resolution up to 400 points while carrying forward the best solution\n3. Bayesian optimization: Use a probabilistic approach to explore the reduced-dimensional search space efficiently\n\nThe rationale is:\n- Symmetry reduction halves the effective search space without sacrificing the integral constraint\n- Multi-resolution leapfrogging escapes local minima by leveraging coarse-to-fine optimization\n- Bayesian optimization balances exploration vs. exploitation in the reduced space\n\nThis approach addresses:\n- The previous program's limited exploration\n- Potential for high local minima trapping\n- Diminished ability to transition between resolutions\n\nKey limits identified:\n- Non-convex optimization means no global guarantee\n- Symmetry reduction drops the effective dimension by ~30%\n- Bayesian acquisition assumes a smooth objective landscape\n\nMathematical specification\n\nRepresentation:  \n- For grid size n (256 <= n <= 400), define h(x) on [0,2] with point spacing dx = 2/n  \n- Symmetry constraint: h(2-x) = 1 - h(x) is enforced by projecting solutions at each optimization step\n- Free variables: the first ceil((n+1)/2) values determine all other values via symmetry\n- Integral constraint: enforced automatically by symmetry (proven by integral arithmetic)\n\nObjective:  \n$$C_5 = \\frac{1}{dx} \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$$  \ncomputed via:  \n- Grid transformation to full correlation length (2*n-1)  \n- Exact, un-averaged correlation matching the quiz evaluator\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Initialization: \n   - If parent solution exists and is symmetric, use it for coarse grid (256 points)\n   - Else start from random valid symmetric solution at 128 points\n\n2. For each grid stage (128, 256, 384, 400):\n   a. Transform parent's sparse representation to current grid\n   b. Project to symmetry and ensure integral conservation\n   c. Add jitter noise for optimization escape\n   d. Run Bayesian optimization with expected improvement acquisition\n   e. Maintain historical best with lower C5\n\n3. Optimization loop:  \n   - Use 50ACQ-SL with symmetry projection at each step  \n   - Define objective as C5 over the transformed grid  \n   - Minimize using reduced dimension (\u224840 variables)  \n   - Break after 200 iterations or improvement stagnation\n\n4. Resolution transition:\n   - Increment grid size by doubling (128\u2192256, 256\u2192512, 512\u2192400)\n   - Project previous best solution using interpolation + symmetry\n   - New optimization inherits this best in the next stage\n\n5. Final polishing:  \n   - Refine the 400-point grid with projected SLSQP  \n   - Re-evaluate with original correlation method\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n1. Grid progression: [128, 256, 384, 400] (powers of 2, then densification)\n2. Bayesian kernel: RBF with automatic relevance detection\n3. Optimization family variation: With each grid stage\n4. Jitter standard deviation: adaptive based on history\n5. Early termination: after 3 consecutive non-improving iterations\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n\n- Stage breakdown: 20% initialization, 30% coarse optimization, 40% grid transition, 10% final polish\n- Feasibility check before grid expansion: ensure previous solution projects cleanly\n- Budget control: each Bayesian optimization step consumes \u22485 sf\n- Fallback: keep best solution from all stages\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify 0<=h[i]<=1 and integral exactly n/2\n- Compute C5 via full correlation match\n- Re-verify the parent's relationship with new interpolation\n- Output: (symmetric_h_values, user_computed_C5, n_points) formatted for quiz",
